Windows SDL 24-bit Macintosh addressing
======================================

Executor selects 24-bit addressing for applications without the
32-bit-compatible SIZE flag. Use -24bit or -32bit to override that choice.
GetMMUMode, SwapMMUMode, StripAddress and Gestalt addressing attributes follow
the active mode. Handles carry their state bits in the master pointer's high
byte in 24-bit mode.

The Windows SDL frontend reserves low guest addresses for callbacks and host
pointer mappings. Host pointers published during startup retain the same guest
address across mode changes. This frontend currently uses the interpreter;
the native translator does not implement these host pointer mappings.

Automatic and forced 24-bit startup default to a 6 MB application heap and a
2 MB system heap. Explicit -memory, -applzone and -syszone options take
precedence. The heap, stack and visible framebuffer must fit below 16 MB.
Larger display modes are rejected if their framebuffer would cross that limit.
Other frontends retain automatic 32-bit startup until they reserve a suitable
framebuffer. Explicit 24-bit startup on them reports the unsupported layout.

CPU regression test (from the workspace root, in WSL):

  cd syn68k
  i686-w64-mingw32-gcc -g -O0 -DHAVE_CONFIG_H -Iinclude \
    test/address24.c runtime/libsyn68k.a -o ../build/address24-test.exe
  ../build/address24-test.exe

The Windows test executes real 68K instructions for tagged byte/word/long
accesses, indexed and absolute addressing, LEA, low-memory reads, PEA and RTE.
It also checks host pointer mappings created before switching to 24-bit mode
and retained after switching back to 32-bit mode. Run the full syn68k CRC
suite as well; make check only builds the Windows harness in this cross-build.

Memory manager regression test (from the workspace root, in WSL):

  bash executor/tests/run-24bit-memory-tests.sh

This links the production memory manager and syn68k library. It checks tagged
handle locking, relocation with retained data and state, 24/32-bit state
conversion, purging, empty master pointers and reallocating empty handles.
Only UI, resource diagnostics and patched-trap callbacks are stubbed.
